---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="MSG wire format"
  description="Byte-level layout of one dialog payload: RESO TOC entry → leading CMDs → UTF-16 body → trailing CMDs → terminator."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Message System', href: `${base}/field-guide/message-system/` },
    { label: 'Wire format' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    A message file is a <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO container</a>
    whose payloads are dialog streams. Each stream is a sequence of
    little-endian 16-bit words. Bit 15 of every word distinguishes <em>control
    tokens</em> (bit 15 clear) from <em>data words</em> (bit 15 set: opcodes,
    arguments, and the UTF-16-LE characters of Japanese text — all of which sit
    above <code>U+8000</code> or are encoded as data above that line).
  </p>

  <h2>Control tokens</h2>
  <p>The parser at ARM9 <code>0x02066c58</code> recognises four control words:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>u16</th><th>ASCII</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>OPEN</code></td><td><code>0x0024</code></td><td><code>$</code></td><td>Start of CMD</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ARG_SEP</code></td><td><code>0x0027</code></td><td><code>'</code></td><td>Argument separator inside a CMD</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>CLOSE</code></td><td><code>0x003b</code></td><td><code>;</code></td><td>End of CMD arg list</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>END</code></td><td><code>0x000c</code></td><td><code>\f</code></td><td>End of dialog payload</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Grammar</h2>
  <pre><code>{`PAYLOAD     := (HEADER_CMD)? CONTENT 0x000C
CONTENT     := (TEXT_RUN | INLINE_CMD | NUL)*
TEXT_RUN    := utf16_word+                       (all words have bit 15 set or are kana/kanji code units)
NUL         := 0x0000                             (phrase boundary; "§" in en_text)
INLINE_CMD  := 0x0024 OPCODE_W (0x0027 ARG_W)* 0x003B (DATA_W* 0x0000)?
HEADER_CMD  := 0x0024 0x8037 (0x0027 ARG_W){1,2} 0x003B 0x0000
OPCODE_W    := u16 with bit 15 set; opcode = (word & 0x7FFF)
ARG_W       := u16 with bit 15 set; arg    = sign_extend15(word & 0x7FFF)
DATA_W      := raw u16 emitted after CLOSE for opcodes that carry a data tail`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>sign_extend15(x)</code> is <code>((int16)(x &lt;&lt; 17)) &gt;&gt; 17</code>
    — that is, the 15-bit value treated as signed.
  </p>

  <h2>Re-emission rules</h2>
  <p>
    From <code>src/translator/reemit.py::cmd_bytes_for_tag</code> — the encoder
    that rebuilds CMD bytes from <code>[TAG]</code> notation:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`bytes  ← <0x0024> <opcode | 0x8000>
for arg in args:
    bytes ← bytes + <0x0027> <(arg & 0x7FFF) | 0x8000>
bytes  ← bytes + <0x003B>
if data_words is not empty:
    for dw in data_words: bytes ← bytes + <dw>
    bytes ← bytes + <0x0000>
`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The trailing <code>0x0000</code> after CLOSE belongs to the CMD only when
    the opcode emits a data tail (e.g. headers, certain script calls).
    A bare CMD with no data words ends at <code>0x003B</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Body text encoding</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Japanese text: UTF-16-LE. All kana/kanji code units fall in
      <code>0x3000+</code>, so they read as data words even though bit 15 is
      clear on some of them — the parser uses the four explicit control
      tokens (above) as the only escape sequence.</li>
    <li><code>§</code> (U+00A7) in the translation database encodes a phrase
      boundary; on the wire it becomes <code>0x0000</code>.</li>
    <li><code>▼</code> (U+25BC) is a "press to advance" page break — emitted
      verbatim into the UTF-16 stream as <code>0x25BC</code>.</li>
    <li>Furigana ruby annotations use literal <code>{`{kanji|reading}`}</code>
      — three ASCII bytes encoded as UTF-16 code units <code>0x007B</code>,
      <code>0x007C</code>, <code>0x007D</code>. The renderer special-cases
      these.</li>
    <li>U+3000 (the full-width space) is the in-game word separator.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Annotated hex dump</h2>
  <p>
    First non-empty entry of <code>data/message/msg00/00/msg00000.ofs</code>
    (excerpted). Words shown LE, two bytes per word, address relative to
    start of the payload:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`offs  bytes        decoded
----  -----------  ---------------------------------------------------
0000  24 00        OPEN                          ┐
0002  18 80        opcode 0x0018  (SPEAKER)      │
0004  27 00        ARG_SEP                       │ inline CMD
0006  72 9b        arg = 0x1B72                  │ opcode=0x18
0008  27 00        ARG_SEP                       │ args=[0x1B72, 0x00]
000A  00 80        arg = 0x0000                  │
000C  3b 00        CLOSE                         ┘
000E  24 00        OPEN                          ┐
0010  17 80        opcode 0x0017  (TEXTBOX)      │
0012  27 00 72 9b  ARG_SEP, arg 0x1B72           │ inline CMD
0016  27 00 b2 80  ARG_SEP, arg 0x0032 (=0xB2&7FFF)│ opcode=0x17
001A  27 00 0a 80  ARG_SEP, arg 0x000A           │ args=[0x1B72,0x32,0x0A,0x01]
001E  27 00 01 80  ARG_SEP, arg 0x0001           │
0022  3b 00        CLOSE                         ┘
0024  24 00 16 80 ... CMD opcode 0x0016 (EXPR), 2 args ...
...
003E  4a 30 84 30   utf-16: お や              (TEXT_RUN starts here)
0042  20 00         space                       (U+0020 — in this file, half-width)
0044  42 30 6a 30 5f 30 ... utf-16: あなた
... (more body text) ...
00A0  bc 25         '▼' page break
... more text + CMDs ...
0180  00 00         NUL (phrase boundary, §)
0182  0c 00         END
`}</code></pre>

  <h2>How the parser walks the stream</h2>
  <p>
    Paraphrased from <code>FUN_02066c58</code> (decompiled from
    <code>arm9_full_plaintext.bin</code>):
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`short *p = msg->text;          // payload pointer (struct field +0x24)
ushort max = msg->len;         // word count       (struct field +0x30)

// 1. optional header — only if first CMD is opcode 0x37
if (*p == 0x0024 && (p[1] & 0x7FFF) == 0x0037) {
    p += 2;
    while (*p != 0x003B && p < end) {
        if (*p == 0x0027) header_arg[i++] = sign_extend15(*++p & 0x7FFF);
        p++;
    }
    if (*p == 0x003B && p[1] == 0) p += 2;     // skip "3B 00"
}

// 2. content loop
for (int line = 0; p < end; ) {
    while (*p != 0x0024 && *p != 0x0000 && *p != 0x000C) p++;
    if (*p == 0x000C) break;                   // END
    if (*p == 0x0000) { p++; continue; }       // phrase boundary

    *p++ = 0;                                  // null-terminate preceding text
    ushort op = *p & 0x7FFF;
    switch (op) {
      case 0x10: line[i].flags = (flags & 0xF0) | 3; break;
      case 0x11: line[i].flags = (flags & 0xF0) | 2; break;
      case 0x12: line[i].flags = (flags & 0xF0) | 1; break;
      case 0x13: line[i].flags &= 0xF0;             break;
      case 0x4C: p += FUN_0206726C(...); continue;  // sub-handler: 2 typed args
      default:   break;                              // renderer handles it
    }
    p++;                                       // advance past opcode
    while (*p == 0x0027) {                     // collect args
        p++; args[k++] = sign_extend15(*p & 0x7FFF); p++;
    }
    if (*p == 0x003B) p++;                     // CLOSE
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The parser only directly interprets six opcodes
    (<code>0x10</code>, <code>0x11</code>, <code>0x12</code>, <code>0x13</code>,
    <code>0x37</code>, <code>0x4C</code>) — everything else is forwarded to the
    renderer <code>func_0x021c0ef8</code> as part of the per-line record.
    See <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/opcodes/`}>opcodes</a>
    for the full catalog.
  </p>

  <h2>Empirical census</h2>
  <p>
    Across 1,328 message files and 50,196 non-empty entries:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>88 distinct opcodes appear.</li>
    <li>2,526 entries open with the <code>0x37</code> HEADER CMD.</li>
    <li>703 of 1,328 files (52.9%) use payload sharing — see
      <a href={`${base}/field-guide/message-system/shared-payloads/`}>shared payloads</a>.</li>
    <li>Top 10 opcodes account for &gt;80% of all CMDs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>src/translator/reemit.py</code> — encoder (CMD bytes, tags, NUL handling)</li>
    <li><code>src/archive/reso.py</code> — TOC parser used to slice payloads from <code>.ofs</code></li>
    <li><code>arm9_full_plaintext.bin</code> @ <code>0x02066c58</code> — parser <code>FUN_02066c58</code></li>
    <li><code>arm9_full_plaintext.bin</code> @ <code>0x021c0ef8</code> — renderer (external symbol)</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
